title: Debugging and watch mode
section: Running
desc: Pause on a failing step, step through a scenario, attach the Node inspector, and re-run on file changes.
lede: When a scenario fails and the error is not enough, run it under the debugger. When you are writing steps, watch mode re-runs what you changed.
---
<h2>Pause when a step fails</h2>
<cb lang="bash">$ npx bddr features/checkout.feature:12 --debug</cb>
<p>bddr runs one scenario at a time, with no retries and no timeouts, and stops after the first failing step. You get a prompt:</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Does</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>c</code></td><td>Continue to the end.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>n</code></td><td>Run the next step, then pause.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>r</code></td><td>Retry the failed step.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>s</code></td><td>Print the scenario state.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>e &lt;expression&gt;</code></td><td>Evaluate an expression with <code>ctx</code> in scope, for example <code>e ctx.scenario.name</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>q</code></td><td>Quit.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><code>--debug-step</code> pauses before every step, not only after a failure. The debugger needs a terminal. Without one it exits with code 2.</p>
<figure class="placeholder" data-kind="screenshot" style="--ratio: 16 / 9">
  <div class="placeholder-frame"><p class="placeholder-what">A terminal paused at a failing step with the debug prompt.</p></div>
  <figcaption>
    <p class="placeholder-how">Capture this yourself:</p>
    <p>In any example project make a step fail on purpose, run it with <code>--debug</code>, type <code>s</code> at the prompt, and capture the terminal showing the failure and the state. Light theme, about 100 columns.</p>
